  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 408 AM CDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\nCurrent surface analysis places low over Oregon into the Idaho\nPanhandle, with high over southern Manitoba into North Dakota \nand northern Minnesota. Upper level analysis places low off the \nPacific Northwest coast while generally zonal flow remains over \nthe Northern Plains. Over our area, quiet weather continues as \nsurface high moves on through. \n\nFor today, high moves off to the east with winds becoming\nsouth-southeasterly in its wake. Otherwise, a quiet day is on\ntap though low humidity values are expected to develop,\nparticularly over parts of western North Dakota. This is also\nwhere winds will be on the increase to around 15 to 25 mph,\ncombining with the low humidity to produce near critical fire\nweather conditions.\n\nOn Tuesday, broad ridging enhances over the midwest nudging into\nthe plains, keeping dry weather over our area but resulting in a\nwarmer temperatures over our area. High temperatures in the mid\n60s to lower 70s can be expected, which are around 10 to 15 \ndegrees above average. \n\nAs we get into Wednesday, aforementioned low drops southerly \noff the west coast, then pushes onshore late Wednesday into \nThursday. In response, ridge amplifies over the plains. This \nwill keep very mild temperatures over our area. \n\nForecast becomes a bit more tricky as we get into the end of the\nweek as another trough off the Pacific Northwest moves onshore\nand interacts with the low. The trouble with latest guidance is\nhow the low lifts up along the trough and the timing with this.\nNevertheless, this brings low to medium precipitation chances \nto our area Friday into Saturday, including the potential for \nsome thunderstorms.",
